# Demo profile for the live schema-change demo (docs/demo/schema-change.md).
#
# It makes the built-in watcher open a room by itself, quickly, so the room
# appears on screen a short time after `devctl demo schema-change` breaks the
# pipeline. The server reads these from its environment; nothing loads this
# file for it. Export them, then start the server:
#
#   bash:        set -a; . dev/demo.env; set +a; go run ./cmd/server
#   PowerShell:  Get-Content dev\demo.env | Where-Object { $_ -match '^[A-Z]' } |
#                  ForEach-Object { $k,$v = $_ -split '=',2; Set-Item "env:$k" $v }
#                go run ./cmd/server
#
# `devctl demo --rehearse` reads this file too, to check it is valid before it
# breaks anything. No secrets here: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY comes from your shell.
#
# The demo resets the pipeline while the server runs; the server keeps its rooms
# because they live in the stack's roomdb service (below), which reset keeps.

# rooms: a firing signal opens (or joins) a room. The default `log` opens none.
PIPEROOM_WATCH_MODE=rooms

# How often the watcher checks. 15s is the minimum the config accepts.
PIPEROOM_WATCH_INTERVAL=15s

# Quiet time after a closed room, so a second run right after closing the first
# room opens a new one. The default is 60m.
PIPEROOM_WATCH_COOLDOWN=30s

# The room database: the stack's roomdb service (`devctl up` starts it; also the
# PIPEROOM_DB_URL default). NOT the pipeline's Postgres on 5432, which `devctl reset`
# wipes; reset keeps roomdb, only `devctl reset --rooms --yes` deletes it (QA G19).
# `devctl demo --rehearse` refuses to run if this points at the pipeline's Postgres.
PIPEROOM_DB_URL=postgres://piperoom:piperoom-dev@localhost:5433/piperoom?sslmode=disable

# Logs for the demo terminal.
PIPEROOM_LOG_LEVEL=info
PIPEROOM_LOG_FORMAT=text

# PIPEROOM_DEV_ACK=dev-login-has-no-password
# Not set on purpose. The server needs it only when dev login listens off
# loopback (PIPEROOM_DEV_ALLOW_NONLOOPBACK=1, e.g. a demo on a shared network);
# the demo listens on 127.0.0.1. If you do expose it, uncomment the line above:
# anyone who can reach the server can then act as any user, including a layer owner.
